From: Webb_S <WebbS@oplc.psb.bls.gov>
Date: Thu, 08 Dec 94 15:25:00 EST
Subject: [#94-12-341] Extropian (?) Fiction

All the posts lately about the basics of anarcho-capitalism has got me 
wondering: what is the Extropian take on Warren Wagar's _A Short History of 
the Future_?  Wagar portrays Earth, Inc. - which sounds something like what 
I imagine an anarcho-capitalist world to be - as a pretty miserable place to 
live.  Of course, things aren't strictly anarchic since governments are 
still around, but in Wagar's future governments are just tools of the 
megacorporations (is this any different from the situation today?) and the 
real power lies with the Global Trade Consortium, an organization developed 
by the top megacorps to keep world capitalism running smoothly.

This also makes me wonder how Extropians feel about the cyberpunk genre of 
literature.  The only cyberpunk novel I've actually read is Gibson's 
_Neuromancer_, but it seems pretty obvious that the world portrayed in 
Gibson's books was faithfully duplicated in the Cyberpunk role-playing game. 
 In the cyberpunk world of 2020, the U.S. still exists, at least on paper, 
but again it's the megacorps which run everything.  If you work for a 
megacorp, they provide you with corporate housing, privately-owned mass 
transit systems to take you between the well-defended housing developments 
and the urban offices, etc.  If you don't work for a megacorp, you're 
basically shit-out-of-luck, unless you like being a street tough laden with 
heavy weaponry and the latest cyberware.

Both Gibson's and Wagar's near-futures seem to me to more closely reflect 
the extropian ideal than today's world.  Personal security is provided by 
private entities (corporations have their own armies) or by the weapons you 
carry.  Corporations can basically do whatever they want because the 
penniless governments are unable to stop them.  There is a _great_ deal of 
personal freedom - basically anything you want, no matter how bizarre, 
dangerous, unethical, or "immoral", is provided by some budding 
entrepreneur.  And so on - I'm sure most of you are familiar with the genre, 
so I won't go into further detail.

When I look at these visions of the future, all I can think is that I would 
rather be dead than live in a place so horrible.  (The cyberpunk RPG doesn't 
refer to it as "Role Playing in the Dark Future" for nothing.)  The private 
firms in these scenarios make life more miserable for the average American 
than Uncle Sam ever could have dreamed.  Is this the world Extropians are 
looking forward to?  Life as a netrunner?  Do extropians see 
anarcho-capitalism as resulting in a different sort of world?  What do you 
find implausible or un-anarchic about these worlds?  I've read many posts by 
people who seem to view governments as savage brutes in comparison to the 
relatively benign private establishments.  I submit that both Wagar and 
Gibson have successfully demonstrated how, if left on their own, capitalists 
can be every bit as horrible as governments.  (Take a look at the tobacco 
industry if you have any lingering doubts.)

On a related note, I've been wondering lately whether were one's willingness 
to embrace an anarcho-capitalist future (which look rather scary to me) has 
anything to do with where one's from.  A recent _Technology Review_ article 
attempted to explain the success of Silicon Valley compared to computer 
companies in the East as (in part, anyway) the result of West Coast 
flexibility vs. New England's desire for stability.  It seems to me that a 
disproportionate number of 'Tropes are from the West Coast.  Is there a 
parallel here?  Are bay area folks more risk-seeking and adaptable than New 
Yorkers such as myself?  Personally, I still dream of living in the little 
house in the suburbs with a wife, 2.3 children, and picket fence (with my 
own quantum-optical desktop supercomputer and nanofactory in the den, of 
course :-)  Does this dream seem laughable to the Silicon Valley computer 
crowd?

